WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
A motor drive switches power electronics.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.640
If its processor computes one wrong value,

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.900
the next PWM edge is wrong too,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.180
and a wrong edge can destroy the bridge.

5
00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:16.045
The usual defence is software self-test.

6
00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:20.465
It runs periodically, and between runs it sees nothing,

7
00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:23.405
so a fault can act inside that gap.

8
00:00:23.296 --> 00:00:25.956
DG32-LITE carries a second,

9
00:00:25.956 --> 00:00:30.176
identical core. CHECKER runs the same instructions two cycles

10
00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:34.256
behind MAIN, on mirrored inputs and bus responses.

11
00:00:34.556 --> 00:00:36.236
Every value MAIN commits,

12
00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:37.916
CHECKER must commit too,

13
00:00:37.916 --> 00:00:38.856
bit for bit.

14
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:42.069
Now a datapath fault changes one store.

15
00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:44.329
CHECKER commits the right value,

16
00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:47.849
and the comparator flags the mismatch on that store,

17
00:00:47.849 --> 00:00:49.969
not at the next self-test.

18
00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:52.969
A latch holds the first cause.

19
00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:54.489
FAULT N goes low,

20
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:57.449
and the gate driver switches the bridge off.

21
00:00:57.909 --> 00:01:02.589
In simulation, injection to latch takes thirty-nine cycles.

22
00:01:03.349 --> 00:01:05.729
No firmware is in that path.

23
00:01:05.768 --> 00:01:08.648
Firmware can still prove the path works.

24
00:01:08.648 --> 00:01:13.608
A locked injection register fires it on purpose,

25
00:01:13.308 --> 00:01:16.668
which is the only way to test it on

26
00:01:16.668 --> 00:01:18.108
real silicon.

27
00:01:18.164 --> 00:01:19.364
Safety in the core.

28
00:01:19.364 --> 00:01:24.564
DG32-LITE goes to first silicon on the September 2026

29
00:01:24.564 --> 00:01:28.824
shuttle; until then, every figure here comes from simulation.
